20261119-17: Add: in Artica Proxy, The block-page service events are shown as a readable timeline: each event says in plain words what happened and how serious it is, with counters, quick filters (errors, access requests, e-mails, starts) and search shortcuts.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The Certificates Center policy of Artica Meta can now hold an internal certificate authority that signs a certificate for each node, the private key never leaving the node.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, The web application firewall status page no longer shows the web service firewall as enabled while it is stopped.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica Proxy, The access requests sent by users from the blocked page are shown as clear cards: the site and page asked for, who asked, why it was blocked, how long the access will last, with a confirmation that says exactly what will be opened.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, Allowing an access request from the console now uses the unblock duration of the error-page rule instead of granting access for ten years.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, Allowing, declining or emptying the access requests now requires a web-filtering or proxy administrator account; read-only accounts only see the list.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, The web service firewall can start again when a backend or an allowed WordPress destination has an IPv6 address: IPv6 entries are left out of the IPv4 allow-list instead of making it fail.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, Restarting the web engine from the service status page no longer leaves the progress bar stuck at 5% or 51%: it now ends with success or failure.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The AI assistant can list the temporary accesses to blocked websites and, after a human approval, remove one or all of them.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica Proxy, The unblock list shows who can open a blocked site right now, how long each access lasts, which ones end within the hour, and lets you search and remove accesses.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, The Unlock button of the blocked page now opens the site for the duration set in the rule; it lasted seconds instead of minutes, and an unlimited unlock expired at once.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, Removing an access from the unblock list, or emptying it, now requires the web filtering administrator privilege; read-only proxy monitors could do it.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica Proxy, The New bandwidth rule window is redesigned: it explains in plain words the shared and per-device limits with examples, offers quick speed presets with their Mbps equivalent, sums up live what the rule will do, warns when no limit is turned on, and opens the new rule on its Proxy objects tab so it can be attached to the websites, networks or users it applies to.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, Creating, changing, enabling, moving or deleting a bandwidth rule now requires the proxy administrator privilege on the server side, not only in the interface.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, A valid SSH public key pasted into the authorized keys form is no longer refused when the copy added invisible characters, carriage returns or line breaks, and a truncated key is now refused instead of being saved as an access that cannot work.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The Details tab of a Docker application is now an identity card: origin, owner, creation and last change dates, version in service with a warning after a rollback, ports and reverse-proxy publications, compose files and profiles, stored secrets with their last change, and the certificate authority with its expiry countdown and a copy button for its fingerprint.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The SSH key files window explains in plain words how to give someone password-free SSH access, shows each account with its key state, lets you download the key file, copy an example sign-in command and replace a lost key after a clear warning.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, Downloading an SSH key file and allowing or blocking sign-in with it now require a full administrator account.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The revisions of a Docker stack are shown as a clear history: the version in service and why it stayed in service when a newer one failed, each version with its author, age and result, the changes of a version opening right under it, and a going-back confirmation that says what restarts and what is kept.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The Internet sharing page now lists active VLANs, shows when sharing is set in the VLAN settings, and lets you turn it on for a VLAN.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The Certificates Center policy of Artica Meta can now have each node request and renew its own Let's Encrypt certificate.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The SSH Authorized Keys window has been redesigned: each key shows its type, fingerprint, comment, account and restrictions, warns about keys open to any network, and lets you choose the account a key opens.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, Deleting an SSH authorized key from the list now asks for confirmation, and editing a key no longer loses its account or its downloadable package.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The Services tab of a Docker application explains each part in plain words (state, self-check, local network access), guides the publication through the reverse-proxy step by step and asks for confirmation in a dedicated dialog before deleting the application.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The masquerading page becomes Internet sharing: each interface shows what is shared, whether it is set here, in the interface settings or by a connector, and asks for confirmation before sharing is turned on or off.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, When the firewall is stopped, an interface whose masquerading has attached firewall rules keeps sharing the Internet connection instead of losing it.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, The masquerading page no longer claims that sharing is unavailable on an interface whose own firewall is disabled; masquerading applies there too.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The Interfaces connectors page is redesigned: it explains with an example why and when to open a path between two network cards, shows each connector as a card with its live state, traffic, options in plain words and any configuration problem, and lets you enable or disable a connector with a switch.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The Certificates Center shows which certificates and trusted authorities are managed by Artica Meta and protects them from local changes.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica Proxy, The blocked page rules tab shows each rule as a readable card with its position, who it covers and what the blocked person can do, with buttons to reorder rules and a permanent default row.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, Turning a blocked page rule on or off now takes effect immediately instead of waiting for the next rule change.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, A blocked page rule now offers one behavior at a time; a rule allowing both unlock and ticket requests showed a ticket in the console while the blocked page offered an unlock.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, Saving the texts, e-mail messages and mail server of the blocked page rules now requires the web filtering administrator privilege.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The DecisionIP firewall events page is redesigned: it explains in plain words which known attackers were stopped, sums up the events shown (sources, most active list, most targeted port), filters them by list in one click, and opens the details of each blocked connection, including for administrators without the web security privilege.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, Artica Meta can now distribute imported certificates and trusted authorities to nodes through the new Certificates Center policy.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, When the captive portal is enabled and the filtering service it depends on has been disabled, the filtering service is now reinstalled automatically, or an alert explains why it cannot run; until now the portal page could not be reached and unauthenticated clients were let through.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, A certificate import from a PEM or ZIP file onto an existing certificate no longer stops the Artica daemon when the database refuses the update; the console shows the error instead.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, Changing the destination of the firewall-to-SIEM link, disconnecting it, switching system logs and forcing a certificate renewal are now recorded in the administrators audit log, and turning system logs off now asks for confirmation.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The "Firewall logs to Artica SIEM" page is redesigned: it explains in plain words what forwarding brings, guides a first connection in three steps, shows the link state, the certificate health with its days left and automatic renewal, the connection test result inline, and a clear switch for system logs.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica Proxy, The redirect rules of the blocked page service are redesigned: each rule reads as a sentence (who, which sites, which visits, where they are sent), the order is numbered, and the page warns when the appliance's blocked page service replaces the addresses of the rules.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, A blocked page redirect rule limited to regular pages (HTTP) or to secure sites (HTTPS) now applies to that kind of visit (the two were swapped), and a rule for every web-filtering rule limited to one category now applies.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica Proxy, The order of the blocked page redirect rules can be changed again, and only web-filtering administrators can add, change or delete them.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica Reverse Proxy, The external security audit page is redesigned: it shows the overall grade and what changed since the previous check, highlights the first things to fix, counts certificates about to expire and domains that do not point to the appliance, and brings back the Analyze now and Send my sites to the audit buttons.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The DNS forward zones page is redesigned: it explains in plain words what a rule does, shows how a name is resolved (your rules first, then the default route with its real servers), lets you pick the kind of rule from four illustrated choices, and hides the add and edit buttons on a cluster slave or a managed appliance, where changes are refused.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The FTP server events page is redesigned: each line says in plain words what happened, who and from where, the successful and failed sign-ins are counted, an address that keeps failing is pointed out, and the search no longer needs a special syntax.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, The FTP server events are read from their absolute log path, so the page no longer stays empty when the Artica daemon runs from another directory.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The FTP accounts page is redesigned: it counts the accounts already used and the ones never used, lets you search and filter them, shows each account's folder, owner and last sign-in, and says before a deletion that the files are kept.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The AI assistant can list the FTP accounts and, after an administrator approves, create, change or delete one; the password is typed by the administrator and never seen by the assistant.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The FTP server page is redesigned: it explains what the service offers, shows its state, memory, accounts and last sign-in, describes each connection setting in plain words, and warns before encryption refuses clients that do not support FTPS.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, The FTP server now applies the folder and file permissions chosen in the console, follows the appliance IPv6 setting (it was inverted), and writes valid log formats and quota queries in its configuration.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, FTP accounts are now managed by the Artica daemon, which validates them and refuses a system folder such as /etc as an account folder; only an administrator can change the FTP server settings.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The Layer 2 switching page has been redesigned: a guided setup checklist, one card per virtual switch with its health, a large confirmation countdown, plain-language port states and a searchable list of devices seen.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica, The RSTP/MSTP service (mstpd) used by the L2 switches is listed in the installed versions and in the package updates, and each new Artica patchset of it is announced.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica Meta, The updates dashboard now reports nodes running an outdated ProFTPD or RSTP/MSTP service (mstpd).
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, Hiding the package updates notification of the top bar now lasts until a new version is published, instead of having no effect.
20261119-17: Fix: in Artica, A removed ProFTPD no longer leaves its old version behind, and no ProFTPD update is offered when the service is enabled but not installed.
20261119-17: Add: in Artica Meta, The node Snapshots window is redesigned: it explains what a snapshot is, shows the node version and whether it is offline, counts snapshots with their total size, tells encrypted from plain files, and spells out what a restore or a deletion changes before you confirm.
